    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d997">

                <pb facs="0904" n="896" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg896"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102F/8: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-08-16T11:00:00+09:00">August 16, 1940—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 17—5:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>702. Reference Peiping’s despatch No. 2460, January 17, 1940.<anchor n="11"
                        xml:id="d997-f1"/> The British Embassy is now authorized to propose to the
                    Japanese Government that a regular steamship service to Sharp Peak [Fukien], be
                    allowed to pass the Japanese blockade.</p>
                <p>Please instruct as to whether the Department desires me to make informal
                    representations to the Foreign Office on humanitarian grounds and because of the
                    need of Americans at Foochow for such service.<anchor n="12" xml:id="d997-f2"
                    /></p>
                <p>Sent to the Department via Peiping.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, repeated to Chungking, Foochow.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d997-f1" n="11" xml:id="d997-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d997-f2" n="12" xml:id="d997-f2-content">
                <p>The Department so instructed in its telegram No. 315, August 19, 6 p.m.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
